LOGBOOK.md-20-- IMPLEMENTATION: Core selects control, ordinary, capped-BDP bulk, and relay control-plus-UDP-burst initial windows with checked configuration and saturating arithmetic. Initial and earned adjustment credit reserve per-channel, per-lane, global-window, and global-ledger capacity before admission; releases are exact and idempotent.
LOGBOOK.md-21-- GATES: Soft caps only new initial windows. Pressure withholds every adjustment and nonessential opens; Critical, rekeying, failed, closing, closed, stale-generation, low-water, and insufficient-reservation paths refuse without changing or queueing credit. Previously advertised credit is never represented as revoked.
LOGBOOK.md-22-- ADAPTER: Both libssh2 `libssh2_channel_open_ex` call sites use one tested initial-window conversion. The adapter still truthfully reports consumer-driven adjustment as unsupported; no protocol message is sent outside that adapter.
LOGBOOK.md:23:- REVIEW REWORK: Revision-1 review found that `reserveAdjustment` treated reserved capacity as one-shot credit and retained an unbounded UUID tombstone set. Adjustment now separates reusable capacity from `newlyReservedBytes`, repeatedly restores outstanding credit to the immutable cap without growing lane/global/ledger totals, rejects impossible outstanding-credit reports, and makes absent release an idempotent zero-release without retaining channel identity.
LOGBOOK.md:24:- VERIFICATION: Twelve policy tests cover tables, 32/64 KiB, capped and overflowing BDP, relay bursts, all budget ceilings, pressure/lane/generation gates, exact lifecycle reconciliation, four repeated replenishments, 10,000 unknown/released-channel churn cycles, and 2,000 deterministic randomized transitions. One selected-adapter test covers all four classes. The full 519-test Swift suite passed with the existing 25 known unavailable-ReluxNIOSSH issues; strict recursive Swift format, core boundaries, diff check, and `swift build` exited 0.
LOGBOOK.md-25-
LOGBOOK.md-26-### Cross-layer memory authority separates reservation from physical evidence (TASK-260715-1pn983)
LOGBOOK.md-27-
LOGBOOK.md-28-- DECISION: One checked-byte ledger owns packet, HEV, SSH, DNS, client relay, rekey, metrics, and reconnect admissions. SSH credit is separate from resident bytes but reserves permitted local storage; failed reads are unknown, never zero or absence.
--
LOGBOOK.md-1147-- COVERAGE: `PacketFlowBridge.swift` plus `PacketFlowBridgeContracts.swift` measure 94.55% line coverage and 88.28% region coverage. Strict Swift format, `swift build`, and the Core boundary guard pass; production sources were not changed.
LOGBOOK.md-1148-- ANOMALY: ThreadSanitizer scheduling exposed a test-observation race where the fake PacketFlow recorded a reverse write before the bridge's subsequent metric increment. The test now waits on the metric boundary explicitly; no product data race or contract defect was found.
LOGBOOK.md-1149-
LOGBOOK.md-1150-### 1208 — libssh2 adapter hits third-round channel/exec/window API boundary (TASK-260715-1ozsb6)
LOGBOOK.md:1151:- BLOCKER: Pinned `ssh2_channel_read` automatically sends WINDOW_ADJUST before returning consumer bytes when remaining credit crosses its private threshold. There is no public manual-window mode, so consumer-earned credit and the immutable advertised cap cannot be enforced.
LOGBOOK.md-1152-- BLOCKER: `ssh2_channel_open` parses all RFC 4254 rejection reasons, then collapses them to `LIBSSH2_ERROR_CHANNEL_FAILURE`; no public typed/numeric reason survives. The remaining diagnostic string is not a stable control-flow API.
LOGBOOK.md-1153-- BLOCKER: Channels store only integer `exit_status` defaulting to zero and an optional signal name. The public API cannot distinguish explicit status 0 from no exit metadata and discards the RFC `core dumped` signal bit.
LOGBOOK.md-1154-- ROUTING: This is the third fork-gap round anticipated by TASK-260720-2sltje. Do not add a partial/mock-only adapter. Recommend failing libssh2 red at M0 unless its value justifies one explicitly approved consolidated fork extension for manual windows, typed open failures, and lossless exec-exit metadata; the alternative is an explicit M0-to-M3/common-contract revision. Exact evidence and resume decision are attached as `TASK-260715-1ozsb6_third-public-api-blocker.md`.
LOGBOOK.md-1155-
--
LOGBOOK.md-1213-
LOGBOOK.md-1214-### 0908 — Review: minimal ReluxNIOSSH fork accepted; two adapter-facing caveats (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
LOGBOOK.md-1215-- MILESTONE: Fork accepted → `done`. Reviewer independently re-ran every gate: `make validate-reluxniossh` (exact archive/license SHA-256, 16-file allowlist, 323 upstream XCTest + 10 Swift Testing, build), strict `swift format lint`, same-pin `conflict-test`, `make validate-core` (49 tests/build) — all exit 0. Default-path equivalence of the new adjust math to upstream verified analytically for even window sizes (default 1<<24); protected-byte boundary matches contract §10.1 (post-encode pre-write at `NIOSSHHandler.swift:266`, post-read pre-decode at `NIOSSHHandler.swift:205`); all outbound frames flow through the single `writeMessage` path.
LOGBOOK.md-1216-- FINDING: During any KEX (client- or server-initiated) upstream's state machine rejects outbound `channelData` with `protocolViolation` (`SSHConnectionStateMachine.swift:1060-1076`), failing the child write promise. Pre-existing upstream behavior, unchanged by the fork — but automatic byte/time rekey makes the collision window likely under bulk traffic. The NIOSSH adapter (TASK-260715-1af33i) MUST gate/queue child writes on `rekeySnapshot.keyExchangeInProgress` / `NIOSSHRekeyStartedEvent`→`NIOSSHRekeySucceededEvent`; the fork exposes exactly this observability for the contract's "writes MAY suspend" clause.
LOGBOOK.md:1217:- FINDING: `NIOSSHChannelWindowConfiguration` invariant is `remaining + buffered + delivered == initialWindowSize`, so remaining credit never grows past `initialWindowSize` even when `maximumWindowSize` is larger, and a `windowAdjustmentThreshold > initialWindowSize` (allowed by preconditions, which only check ≤ max) permanently withholds WINDOW_ADJUST — a stalled-channel footgun. Adapter policy must keep threshold ≤ initial (≤ half is sane). Cap semantics remain contract-correct: `remainingBefore + adjustment ≤ cap` always holds.
LOGBOOK.md-1218-- NOTE: `.serverInitiated` rekey reason semantically means "remote-initiated" (a server-role handler labels client-triggered KEX the same way); cosmetic for the client-only tunnel. `requestRekey` in a non-active state fails the promise and also fires `errorCaught` — callers should not treat pre-active requests as recoverable no-ops.
LOGBOOK.md-1219-
LOGBOOK.md-1220-### 0850 — Minimal ReluxNIOSSH window/rekey fork is reviewable (TASK-260715-nzdzv3)
LOGBOOK.md-1221-- DECISION: The source fork is an independent SwiftPM package at `Dependencies/ReluxNIOSSH`, based byte-for-byte on SwiftNIO SSH `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c` before a 16-file allowlisted delta. The `NIOSSH` product/module and upstream 16 MiB/no-auto-rekey defaults remain source-compatible; `ReluxTunnelCore` has no dependency on the fork.
--
LOGBOOK.md-1233-- NOTE: Audit's NIOSSH release dates are tag-commit dates, not GitHub `published_at`; tag dates match upstream exactly, no correction needed. Reviewer evidence: board resource `TASK-260715-28ok1k_review-verdict.md`.
LOGBOOK.md-1234-
LOGBOOK.md-1235-### 0759 — SSH engine source audit pins fixed baselines without selecting an engine (TASK-260715-28ok1k)
LOGBOOK.md-1236-- SECURITY: SwiftNIO SSH is pinned to `0.14.1` / `31cdc3c`, which fixes critical CVE-2026-43798, with resolved Swift Crypto `4.5.1` and SwiftNIO `2.101.3` security-patched transitives. libssh2 release `1.11.1` is not a clean baseline because six advisories were fixed after release; the candidate is pinned to untagged `1.11.2_DEV` commit `a343024` containing all six fixes, with OpenSSL `3.5.7`. Re-audit on a signed libssh2 1.11.2+ release is mandatory.
LOGBOOK.md:1237:- FINDING: SwiftNIO SSH hardcodes each child receive-window target to 16 MiB and exposes client `_rekey()` only internally. libssh2 exposes generic per-channel initial/remaining/adjust APIs, but its direct-tcpip helper fixes the 2 MiB default and it likewise has no public client-rekey API or automatic byte/time threshold.
LOGBOOK.md-1238-- DECISION: ADR-014 remains open. Both candidates retain identical host-key, window, sustained 5 GiB rekey, scale (through 1,000 channels), cancellation, algorithm, extension-build, memory, and physical-size gates. Unknowns are named experiments in `.research/260720_task-260715-28ok1k-ssh-engine-candidate-audit.md`; no missing capability was waived.
LOGBOOK.md-1239-- RISK: libssh2 master issue #2023 contains an unresolved AEAD correctness report whose specific leak interpretation is disputed. Treat it as an ASan/packet-content experiment, not a proven leak or a reason to prejudge the engine.
